<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Central African Republic</h1><p class="st-meta">1960 CE – present · capital Bangui</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A landlocked republic in Central Africa, independent from France in 1960, with coups, Bokassa's empire and a civil war since 2012.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ubangi-shari">Ubangi-Shari</a> was granted autonomy on 1 December 1958 as the Central African Republic under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barthelemy-boganda">Barthélemy Boganda</a>, its first prime minister. After Boganda died in a plane crash on 29 March 1959, David Dacko took control of his party, MESAN, and became the first president when the country received independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> on 13 August 1960.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Dacko suppressed opposition parties and declared MESAN the official party of the state in 1962. Colonel Jean-Bédel Bokassa overthrew him on 31 December 1965, named himself Emperor Bokassa I of the Central African Empire on 4 December 1976, and was overthrown with French help in September 1979, when Dacko was restored. General André Kolingba took power in a coup in 1981. After pressure for reform, Ange-Félix Patassé won a second round of elections in 1993, and François Bozizé overthrew him in March 2003. The Central African Bush War began in 2004.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>Rebels of the Séléka coalition seized the capital in March 2013 and Michel Djotodia took over as president, after which Séléka fighters and Christian anti-balaka militias fought, and the country was de facto partitioned by the end of 2014. Faustin-Archange Touadéra won the 2016 election and was reelected in December 2020 and in December 2025, after a 2023 referendum abolished constitutional term limits.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/central-african-republic.jpg" alt="Central African Republic" width="330" height="220"><figcaption>Image: Barthélemy Boganda · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Central_African_Republic.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1960 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bangui</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Barthélemy Boganda, David Dacko, Jean-Bédel Bokassa, André Kolingba, Ange-Félix Patassé</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity (Protestant and Roman Catholic), Islam, indigenous beliefs, mainly</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French, Sango</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/central-african-republic">Central African Republic</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Central African Empire</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1993&amp;focus=central-african-republic" aria-label="Central African Republic on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: where Central African Republic lay"><image href="/maps/globes/g03000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4031 4626L4031 4626"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4031 4626h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4031 4626h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4031 4626h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Bangui. <a href="/atlas?year=1993&amp;focus=central-african-republic">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
